Thanks for this answer!  Let me try to wring one last answer out of you.

Suppose I have twelve items. Items 1-6 measure my theoretical factor 1, items 7-12 measure my theoretical factor, and my goal is to compare the distribution of factor scores. Now as I said, I could use a bifactorial model, but my theory does not necessarily include the existence of one overarching factor. So now, I do a model with 0-slope-constraints (items 1-6 have 0-slope for factor 2, items 7-12 have 0-slope for factor 1, as specified in my initial question).  I could now compare factor sores, but am not sure if this is valid because the non-factor specific difficulty (or location) parameter carries information on the items also. So do you think I can go with this approach, or should I impose additional constrains (I have in mind equal location-, or difficulty-parameters across all items)?

I hope this makes sense!

Thanks again,
Tobi

