On Tue, Oct 14, 2014 at 8:42 PM, Tobias Konitzer <tobias....@gmail.com> wrote:
> Ah, that is too bad. What comparisons of factor-scores are you having in mind, if mean-variance comparisons are obviously ruled out?

Usually I don't recommend factor-score comparisons since they are biased, and require computations to be done in two-stages (which is always less optimal). That's why I was suggesting ways earlier to do these applications by manipulating the model parameters directly and forming hypotheses there.

> Also ,what do you mean by "model based comparison"?

Anything based on the model log-likelihood and it's curvature. So Wald tests, likelihood ratio tests, information statistics, etc.

> I suppose scaling factors in separate models means I cannot compare scores at all. Also, do you know of a good statistical resource that goes a little more deeply into these issues of estimation than your JStatSoft piece?

I don't really, but getting familiar with structural equation modeling (which is much more popular) is a good start since confirmatory factor analysis in that area (especially with categorical indicators) is extremely similar to multidimensional IRT. Cheers.

Phil

>
> One big thanks for all your help (and a fantastically maintained and well documented R-package),
> Tobi
