Hi Phil,

I have been thinking more about the Rasch approach. If I want to compare the intercepts after constraining the item-slopes, my impression would be to run a one-dimensional model and then run the wald-test checking whether the first six items are statistically more significant in determining the latent traut. Is this correct?

Second, using the wald-test, I have problems with the information-matrix L. I would want to check whether parameters 2,6,10,14,18 and 22 are significantly different from parameters 26,30,34,38, 42 and 46. But how would I specify my L?

Third, after running the rasch model, I notice that all the intercepts are negative (and, of course, all slopes are equal to 1).  I am under the impression that the bigger the intercept (e.g. in my case the closer the intercept is to 0),  the more "important" is the item for the underlying latent trait. Is this correct?

Many thanks again, also for a great, great package!
Tobi

